<section
  id='case-portfolio-logotype'
  class='fullscreen-section case-study-section'
  data-title='Case Study: KT Portfolio — Logo Design | Katja Turnsek'
  data-description='Graphic design case study for a hand-drawn KT mark refined into a fluid, flexible identity for digital and print use.'
  data-case='portfolio'
  aria-labelledby='case-portfolio-logotype-title'
>
  <div class='rows'>
    <div class='row'>
      <div class='case-study-header'>
        <p class='case-eyebrow'>Graphic Design</p>
        <h1 id='case-portfolio-logotype-title'>KT Portfolio</h1>
        <p class='case-subtitle'>
          A hand-drawn initials mark developed into a personal identity for my design and front-end
          work.
        </p>

        <dl class='case-summary' aria-label='Project summary'>
          <div>
            <dt>Role</dt>
            <dd>Identity and logo design</dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt>Scope</dt>
            <dd>Independent portfolio project</dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt>Year</dt>
            <dd>2025–2026</dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt>Tools</dt>
            <dd>Pencil and paper, Adobe Illustrator</dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt>Outcome</dt>
            <dd>Flexible personal logo suite</dd>
          </div>
        </dl>

        <div class='tech-demo-wrapper case-demo-wrapper'>
          <div class='demo-links' data-auto='demo' data-case='portfolio-logotype'></div>
        </div>

        <nav
          class='project-switcher'
          aria-label='KT Portfolio sections'
          data-auto='switcher'
          data-case='portfolio'
        ></nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class='row'>
      <h2 class='case-section-heading'>The brief</h2>
      <p>
        I wanted my personal identity to bring together the two sides of my work: visual design and
        front-end development. It needed to feel creative and handmade while fitting naturally into
        a portfolio built around organic shapes, movement, and illustrated details. The mark also
        had to work as a small navigation logo as well as part of wider layouts.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class='row'>
      <div class='case-study-grid'>
        <article class='case-study-block case-study-block--challenge'>
          <h2>Challenge</h2>
          <p>
            Combining K and T into one irregular shape made the mark distinctive, but it also
            created a risk that the initials would disappear when the logo was reduced. The main
            challenge was keeping the character of the original sketch while making the silhouette
            and negative space clear enough for practical use.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li>Combine both initials without creating a formal monogram</li>
            <li>
              Keep the hand-drawn movement while improving clarity and balance
            </li>
            <li>
              Make the identity work as an icon, wordmark, and reversed logo
            </li>
          </ul>
        </article>

        <figure class='case-study-item case-study-media--explore'>
          <img
            class='thumb'
            src='{{base}}assets/images/logo-ktportfolio-600.webp'
            data-src='logo-ktportfolio-600.webp'
            data-path='assets/images'
            width='600'
            height='461'
            loading='lazy'
            decoding='async'
            alt='Pencil explorations of a fluid KT monogram beside the refined black vector logo'
          />
          <figcaption class='case-caption'>
            Early pencil ideas beside the refined KT symbol
          </figcaption>
        </figure>

        <article class='case-study-block case-study-block--decisions'>
          <h2>Key decisions</h2>
          <ul>
            <li>
              I began with loose pencil sketches so I could explore how the initials might flow into
              one shape before making the design more precise.
            </li>
            <li>
              The ink-like silhouette connects the identity to my background in traditional art and
              to the organic visual language of the portfolio.
            </li>
            <li>
              I simplified the outer contour and adjusted the internal spaces so the K and T remain
              visible when the mark is used at navigation and icon sizes.
            </li>
            <li>
              I created icon-only, horizontal, vertical, monochrome, outline, and reversed versions
              for different placements and backgrounds.
            </li>
          </ul>
        </article>

        <figure class='case-study-item case-study-media--booking'>
          <img
            class='thumb'
            src='{{base}}assets/images/design-portfoliologosuite-600.webp'
            data-src='design-portfoliologosuite-600.webp'
            data-path='assets/images'
            width='600'
            height='424'
            loading='lazy'
            decoding='async'
            alt='KT Portfolio logo suite showing primary, secondary, icon, vertical, outline, monochrome, and reversed variations'
          />
          <figcaption class='case-caption'>
            Logo variations for navigation, wider layouts, and contrasting backgrounds
          </figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class='row case-outcome'>
      <h2 class='case-section-heading'>Outcome and reflection</h2>
      <p>
        The finished mark gives the portfolio a personal signature without using a typical design or
        coding symbol. It keeps the movement and irregular character of the original sketches while
        the different variations make it practical across the website and other formats. Designing
        it for my own portfolio also reminded me that an expressive logo still needs to be tested in
        the small, everyday places where people will see it most often.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class='back-to-work-container'>
      <a
        href='{{base}}#work'
        class='label-link back-to-work-label'
        data-back
        data-back-fallback='{{base}}#work'
        aria-label='Back to Work'
      >
        <svg class='icon icon-18 arrow-icon' aria-hidden='true'>
          <use href='#i-arrow-left' />
        </svg>
        <span class='label-text'>Back to Work</span>
      </a>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>